<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="hydroxycinnamate sugar acid ester biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>Hydroxycinnamate (HCA) esters are frequently found in a broad array across the plant kingdom and can form, for instance up to 20 different HCA conjugates in &lt;i&gt;Secale cereale&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[Strack86]|. The formation of HCA esters may proceed via three different routes, one of them includes the utilization of HCA-CoA thioesters and the other one exploits HCA-glucosides such as |FRAME:P-COUMAROYL-COA|, |FRAME:FERULOYL-COA| and |FRAME:SINAPOYL-COA| (this pathway), whereas the third route proceeds via HCA-quinate such as |FRAME:CAFFEOYLQUINATE| (see |FRAME:PWY-6673|). The pathway depicted above points out that both biosynthetic routes may occur within one plant, i.e. &lt;i&gt;Cestrum elegans&lt;/i&gt; forming the exact same product (|FRAME: O-SINAPOYLGLUCARATE|) |CITS:[Strack88a]|. It might be reasonable to assume that the formed HCA conjugates accumulate outside the protoplast in the cell walls as HCA conjugates have not been found within mesophyll protoplasts |CITS:[Schulz86]| and are known to form aggregates with cell wall bound carbohydrates |CITS:[Harris80]|. The pathway describes the formation of HCA conjugates, i.e. |FRAME: O-SINAPOYLGLUCARATE| and |FRAME: O-SINAPOYLGLUCAROLACTONE|, using various activated sugar acids such as |FRAME:SINAPOYL-COA| and |FRAME:1-O-SINAPOYL-BETA-D-GLUCOSE| as acyl donors. It should be noted that the enzymes of the pathway accept |FRAME:P-COUMAROYL-COA| and |FRAME:FERULOYL-COA| with a comparable turnover rate but are more specific towards their acyl-acceptors such as |FRAME:D-GLUCARATE| and |FRAME:CPD-12595| which results in the observed variety of HCA conjugates in the respective plants |CITS:[Strack87a] [Strack88a]|.</Description>
